  <h2><span class="secno">3</span>The signal, replicated and sharpened</h2>
  <p>Our independent pipeline reproduces the phenomenon precisely. Maximizing the exponential sum over the
  2.96M-term dataset gives</p>
  <div class="kbox">
    <div class="kpi"><div class="v">2.5714474984</div><div class="l">λ (this work, N = 2.96×10⁶)</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="v">2.4434429678<span class="u">302</span></div><div class="l">2π/λ — agrees with the
      Gibbs–McCranie trillion-scale value 2.443442967784743 to ten significant digits</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="v">{2, 3, 47, 69}</div><div class="l">complete list of elements with
      cos(λa) ≥ 0 below 4×10⁷ — identical to Steinerberger's list</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="v">99.9885<span class="u">%</span></div><div class="l">of elements lie in the
      middle third ρ ∈ [⅓, ⅔], where ρ = λa/2π mod 1</div></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="wide">
  <figure>
    <div id="fig1" class="vz"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Figure 1</b> The hidden signal. Each dot is an Ulam number a<sub>n</sub> (log-spaced sample
    of 1,066 from the 2,959,301 below 4×10⁷), plotted by index n against its circle coordinate
    ρ = λa<sub>n</sub>/2π mod 1. The sequence confines itself to the shaded middle third of the circle.
    Orange marks the outliers — the 340 elements outside [⅓, ⅔] — which press ever closer to the window edges
    as n grows; the four labelled elements {2, 3, 47, 69} are the only ones in the outer half, and
    §5 shows they are also the sequence's four principal generators. Hover for values.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<div class="col">
  <p>The support tightens toward exactly [⅓, ⅔]: the interval holding all but 10⁻⁴ of the mass is
  [0.3336, 0.6661] at this scale, and every one of the 340 outliers found beyond n ≈ 100 sits within 0.03 of
  an edge (distance to edge vs. log value: r = −0.65). This is Gibbs's edge-convergence conjecture
  <a href="#ref-gibbs"><sup class="fn">[4]</sup></a> in quantitative form.</p>
  <p>The Fourier spectrum of the set contains, at every amplitude we can resolve, only the harmonic comb
  {kλ mod 2π}: the twelve largest peaks below the Nyquist frequency are, in order, k = 1, 5, 4, 6, 2, 3, 9,
  8, 7, 10, 11, 13. We sharpened this test by computing the spectrum separately for each gap-class — the
  positions whose following gap is 2, 3, 17, 20, 25, 12, 22, 15 — and found the same comb and nothing else in
  each subpopulation. Whatever hidden state the sequence carries beyond θ (and §4 shows something must be),
  it is not a second rotation: there is no other frequency to find, even class-resolved.</p>
  <p>The window density itself (Figure 2) is not uniform: mass piles against the interior of both edges and
  dips at the centre — the shape behind Steinerberger's harmonic coefficients. The mean drift per term is
  λ/d mod 2π = 0.528 of a full circle, so consecutive terms land on alternating sides of the window: the
  sequence sweeps the window in a two-phase gait, which is where the strong negative correlation of adjacent
  gaps (§4) begins.</p>
</div>

<div class="wide">
  <figure>
    <div id="fig2" class="vz"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Figure 2</b> The invariant window profile: kernel of the phenomenon. Density of
    ρ = λa/2π mod 1 over all 2.96M elements (200 bins). 99.99% of mass lies inside the middle third
    (shaded); the profile is stable from N = 10⁵ on and is, by §4, the marginal of the sequence's
    effective hidden state.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<div class="col">
  <h2><span class="secno">4</span>How much does the signal know?</h2>
  <p>The natural symbolic encoding of the sequence is its gap process g<sub>n</sub> = a<sub>n+1</sub> −
  a<sub>n</sub>. The gaps use a remarkably small alphabet — 114 distinct values across 2.96M steps, growing
  roughly logarithmically (99 at N = 7.4×10⁵), with no gap exceeding 587 anywhere below 4×10⁷ — and a
  first-order entropy of H(g) = 3.35 bits. Because θ<sub>n+1</sub> = θ<sub>n</sub> + λg<sub>n</sub> mod 2π
  exactly, the pair (θ<sub>n</sub>, g<sub>n</sub>) is the natural state-observation pair: <em>if</em> the
  Ulam rule were secretly a function of the circle coordinate, g<sub>n</sub> would be determined by
  θ<sub>n</sub> and the sequence would be an interval-exchange-like dynamical system. It is not — but it is
  surprisingly close, in a specific sense.</p>

  <h3><span class="secno">4.1</span>The signal carries half the local information</h3>
  <p>Conditioning the next gap on the circle coordinate removes
  I(g<sub>n</sub>; θ<sub>n</sub>) = <strong>1.61 bits — 48.2%</strong> of H(g). The estimate saturates under
  refinement of the θ-partition (1.36, 1.58, 1.62, 1.63, 1.64 bits at 16, 64, 256, 1024, 4096 bins), so
  1.6 bits is a property of the coordinate, not of a discretization. A maximum-likelihood guess of the next
  gap succeeds 36.9% of the time from the marginal and 59.4% from θ. The conditional structure is vividly
  banded (Figure 3): each gap value is <em>phase-locked</em> to a sub-interval of the window, as it must be —
  a gap g is geometrically feasible at θ only if θ + λg mod 2π lands back inside the window — but the bands
  are graded rather than sharp, and no refinement of θ makes them deterministic.</p>
</div>

<div class="wide">
  <figure>
    <div id="fig3" class="vz"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Figure 3</b> Phase-locking of gaps to the hidden coordinate: P(g<sub>n</sub> | ρ<sub>n</sub>)
    for the ten most common gaps (rows, 76% of all steps; ordered by wrapped step λg mod 2π), over 96 bins of
    ρ across the window. Ink depth is conditional probability (sequential scale); the grey strip on top shows
    where the sequence actually spends its time. Each gap operates from its own phase territory — but within
    a territory the choice of gap remains genuinely uncertain. Hover any cell for values.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<div class="col">
  <h3><span class="secno">4.2</span>θ is an approximate sufficient statistic — the screening property</h3>
  <p>Adjacent gaps are strongly dependent: I(g<sub>n</sub>; g<sub>n−1</sub>) = 1.35 bits. Conditioned on the
  circle coordinate, this dependence all but vanishes:</p>
  <p style="text-align:center" class="mono">
  I(g<sub>n</sub>; g<sub>n−1</sub> | θ<sub>n</sub>) = 0.088 bits — a <strong>93.5% reduction</strong>.</p>
  <p>Almost everything one gap tells you about the next is <em>routed through the circle</em>. In the language
  of state-space models, θ acts as an approximate sufficient statistic for one-step prediction; the gap
  process is close to a hidden-Markov process whose hidden state is a point on the circle advancing by
  λg per step. We regard this as the report's central new fact about the signal: it upgrades the signal from
  a distributional curiosity (a non-uniform marginal) to the near-complete <em>mediator of the sequence's
  short-range dynamics</em>. The residual 0.088 bits is small but robustly nonzero (stable across bin counts
  and both dataset halves), and at lags 2–5 the θ-conditioned dependence (~0.2 bits) actually exceeds its
  unconditioned counterpart — conditioning on a common intermediate reveals, as it should, that some
  additional slow state exists beyond the circle.</p>

  <h3><span class="secno">4.3</span>What the signal does not know</h3>
  <p>How much structure is there in total? Deepening the context, the conditional entropy of the next gap
  falls without reaching a floor we can certify:</p>
</div>

<div class="wide">
  <figure>
    <div id="fig4" class="vz"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Figure 4</b> The information ladder. Left: conditional entropy of the next gap under
    increasing conditioning (bits/gap; split-half bias-corrected plug-in). The single circle coordinate
    (accented bar) is worth as much as the two preceding gaps combined; but deeper context keeps digging below
    it, to ~0.91 bits at depth 6, with LZMA on the whole 2.96M-symbol gap sequence achieving 1.00 bits/gap.
    A density-matched random set would cost 5.14 bits/gap (dashed marker, axis clipped). Right: the screening
    measurement of §4.2. Hover bars for exact values.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<div class="col">
  <p>Three reference points calibrate the ladder. A memoryless random set of the same density would cost
  H₂(d)/d = 5.14 bits/gap: the Ulam sequence is about <strong>5× more compressible than random</strong>.
  A deterministic sequence would reach 0: the observed ~0.9–1.0 bits/gap of apparent entropy at depth 6
  (and under a general-purpose compressor with unbounded context) is what any finite-order model of the
  Ulam sequence currently fails to explain — about 0.07 bits per integer. And the circle coordinate's
  1.6 bits sit almost exactly at the level of two gaps of exact history — H(g|θ) = 1.735 vs.
  H(g|g₁,g₂) = 1.730 — a coincidence worth a conjecture (§7). The sequence is emphatically not
  “quasi-random”: it is five-sixths order; but the remaining sixth has, so far, the signature of genuine
  randomness rather than of an undiscovered second frequency (§3).</p>

  <h3><span class="secno">4.4</span>The residue diffuses: no bounded remainder</h3>
  <p>If the Ulam sequence were a model set — a cut-and-project quasicrystal, the cleanest structure
  compatible with a circle window — its counting function would track d·x with bounded error. It does not.
  The variance of m-step block sums S<sub>m</sub> = g<sub>n+1</sub>+⋯+g<sub>n+m</sub> grows as
  m<sup>0.95±0.03</sup> across three decades (Figure 5): asymptotically linear diffusion, with an
  anti-correlation-suppressed diffusion constant of ≈ 0.28–0.35 × Var(g). Equivalently the deviation
  e<sub>n</sub> = a<sub>n</sub> − n/d executes a slow random walk (its spectrum is a featureless low-frequency
  continuum — no discrete line, matching the class-resolved comb search of §3). Combined with §4.2 this gives
  the report's structural synthesis: <em>a rigid single-frequency skeleton, occupied stochastically</em>.
  The window and its profile are deterministic and invariant; which window slots the sequence takes drifts
  diffusively, carrying positive apparent entropy. “Stochastic quasicrystal” seems to us the accurate name,
  and it explains simultaneously why the sequence looks so ordered (Fourier sees the skeleton) and why its
  density has resisted proof (the occupancy process is not finitely determined by the visible state).</p>
</div>

<div class="wide">
  <figure>
    <div id="fig5" class="vz"></div>
    <figcaption><b>Figure 5</b> Diffusive occupancy. Left: Var(S<sub>m</sub>)/(m·Var g) vs. block length m
    (log–log x). An i.i.d. process sits at 1 (upper line); a bounded-remainder set decays like 1/m (grey
    guide); the Ulam sequence descends through short-range anti-correlation and then flattens near 0.3 —
    linear-in-m variance, H ≈ 0.48. Right: the deviation a<sub>n</sub> − n/d across the full dataset —
    a random walk of amplitude ±6×10³ over 3M terms, not a bounded fluctuation.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>
